window.A.registerBank({"chapter": 4, "title": "Equations, inequalities and graphs", "syllabusOutcomes": {"4.1": "Solve modulus equations: |ax+b|=c, |ax+b|=cx+d, |ax+b|=|cx+d|, |ax²+bx+c|=d (algebraic or graphical)", "4.2": "Solve modulus inequalities: k|ax+b|>c, k|ax+b|⩽c, k|ax+b|⩽|cx+d|, |ax+b|⩽cx+d, |ax²+bx+c|>d, |ax²+bx+c|⩽d", "4.3": "Use a substitution to form and solve a quadratic equation in order to solve a related equation", "4.4": "Sketch cubic polynomials and their moduli, given as a product of three linear factors", "4.5": "Solve cubic inequalities f(x) ⩾ d (etc.) graphically, where f is a product of three linear factors"}, "groups": [{"key": "mod-eq", "label": "Modulus equations", "outcomes": ["4.1"]}, {"key": "mod-ineq", "label": "Modulus inequalities", "outcomes": ["4.2"]}, {"key": "mod-quad-graph", "label": "Modulus-of-quadratic graphs", "outcomes": ["4.1", "4.2"]}, {"key": "subst", "label": "Substitution → quadratic", "outcomes": ["4.3"]}, {"key": "cubic", "label": "Cubic sketches & inequalities", "outcomes": ["4.4", "4.5"]}, {"key": "graph-read", "label": "Reading given graphs", "outcomes": ["4.4", "4.5"]}], "questions": [{"id": "s26-p11-q1", "group": "mod-eq", "source": {"session": "May/June 2026", "paper": "4037/11", "question": "1"}, "calc": false, "outcomes": ["4.1"], "topics": ["|ax+b| = |cx+d|"], "marks": 3, "targetMinutes": 5, "difficulty": 1, "statement": "<p>Solve the equation \\(\\;|5x+4| = |3x-2|\\).&emsp;<b>[3]</b></p>", "tips": ["\\(|A|=|B|\\) means the two expressions are equal in size: either \\(A = B\\) or \\(A = -B\\). Write <b>both</b> equations — no other cases exist.", "Alternative: square both sides — \\(|A|=|B| \\iff A^2 = B^2\\) — then solve the quadratic.", "Always solve <b>two</b> linear equations and expect (up to) two answers. One equation only earns M0."], "solution": ["<p><b>Case 1 (same sign):</b> \\(5x+4 = 3x-2 \\Rightarrow 2x = -6 \\Rightarrow x = -3\\).</p>", "<p><b>Case 2 (opposite sign):</b> \\(5x+4 = -(3x-2) = -3x+2 \\Rightarrow 8x = -2 \\Rightarrow x = -\\tfrac14\\).</p>", "<p><b>Check</b> (non-calculator, quick): \\(x=-3:\\; |{-11}| = |{-11}|\\) ✓. \\(x=-\\tfrac14:\\; |\\tfrac{11}{4}| = |-\\tfrac{11}{4}|\\) ✓</p>", "<p><i>Alternative:</i> square both sides: \\((5x+4)^2=(3x-2)^2 \\Rightarrow 16x^2+52x+12=0 \\Rightarrow 4x^2+13x+3=0 \\Rightarrow (4x+1)(x+3)=0\\), same answers.</p>"], "answer": "\\(x = -3\\) or \\(x = -\\tfrac14\\)", "markScheme": ["M1 — states BOTH equations: \\(5x+4=3x-2\\) and \\(5x+4=-3x+2\\) (or squares both sides to \\(16x^2+52x+12=0\\)) with an attempt to solve", "A1 — \\(x=-3\\)", "A1 — \\(x=-\\tfrac14\\)"]}, {"id": "s25-p11-q5a", "group": "mod-eq", "source": {"session": "May/June 2025", "paper": "4037/11", "question": "5(a)"}, "calc": false, "outcomes": ["4.1"], "topics": ["k|ax+b| + c = d"], "marks": 3, "targetMinutes": 5, "difficulty": 1, "statement": "<p>Solve the equation \\(\\;5\\,|2x-1| + 8 = 23\\).&emsp;<b>[3]</b></p>", "tips": ["<b>Isolate the modulus first</b>: get \\(|2x-1|\\) alone on one side before splitting into cases.", "\\(|A| = c\\) (with \\(c>0\\)) splits into \\(A = c\\) or \\(A = -c\\).", "Never distribute the 5 \"into\" the modulus while it still has the +8 attached — isolate, then split."], "solution": ["<p>Isolate the modulus: \\(5|2x-1| = 15 \\Rightarrow |2x-1| = 3\\).</p>", "<p>Split: \\(2x-1 = 3 \\Rightarrow x = 2\\), or \\(2x-1 = -3 \\Rightarrow x = -1\\).</p>", "<p><b>Check:</b> \\(x=2: 5|3|+8 = 23\\) ✓; \\(x=-1: 5|-3|+8=23\\) ✓</p>"], "answer": "\\(x = 2\\) or \\(x = -1\\)", "markScheme": ["M1 — isolates and splits: \\(2x-1=3\\) and \\(2x-1=-3\\)", "A2 — \\(x=2\\) and \\(x=-1\\) (A1 for either correct)", "Alt: \\(4x^2-4x-8=0\\) (B1), solves 3-term quadratic (M1), both answers (A1)"]}, {"id": "s24-p21-q1", "group": "mod-eq", "source": {"session": "May/June 2024", "paper": "4037/21", "question": "1"}, "calc": true, "outcomes": ["4.1"], "topics": ["graph of |linear|", "|ax+b| = |cx+d|"], "marks": 5, "targetMinutes": 8, "difficulty": 2, "parts": [{"label": "(a)", "text": "On the axes, sketch the graph of \\(y = |4x-6|\\), showing the coordinates of the points where the graph meets the coordinate axes.", "marks": 2}, {"label": "(b)", "text": "Solve the equation \\(|4x-6| = |2x|\\).", "marks": 3}], "statement": "<p><b>(a)</b> On the axes, sketch the graph of \\(y = |4x-6|\\), showing the coordinates of the points where the graph meets the coordinate axes.&emsp;<b>[2]</b></p><p><b>(b)</b> Solve the equation \\(\\;|4x-6| = |2x|\\).&emsp;<b>[3]</b></p>", "tips": ["To sketch \\(y=|mx+c|\\): draw the line \\(y=mx+c\\), then reflect the part below the \\(x\\)-axis upwards. You get a V with its vertex on the \\(x\\)-axis.", "The vertex is where \\(4x-6=0\\Rightarrow x=1.5\\); the \\(y\\)-intercept is \\(|{-6}|=6\\). <b>Label both</b> — the marks are for the labelled intercepts.", "For (b): \\(|A|=|B|\\Rightarrow A=B\\) or \\(A=-B\\) (two linear equations), or square both sides."], "solution": ["<p><b>(a)</b> The line \\(y=4x-6\\) crosses the axes at \\((0,-6)\\) and \\((1.5,0)\\). Taking the modulus folds the negative part up: V-shape with vertex \\((1.5, 0)\\) and \\(y\\)-intercept \\((0, 6)\\).</p>", {"figure": {"x": [-1, 4], "y": [-1, 8], "xTicks": 1, "yTicks": 2, "curves": [{"poly": [4, -6], "abs": true}], "points": [[1.5, 0, "(1.5, 0)"], [0, 6, "(0, 6)"]]}}, "<p><b>(b)</b> Case 1: \\(4x-6 = 2x \\Rightarrow 2x = 6 \\Rightarrow x = 3\\).<br>Case 2: \\(4x-6 = -2x \\Rightarrow 6x = 6 \\Rightarrow x = 1\\).</p>", "<p><b>Check:</b> \\(x=3: |6|=|6|\\) ✓; \\(x=1: |-2|=|2|\\) ✓</p>"], "answer": "(a) V-graph, vertex \\((1.5,0)\\), \\(y\\)-int \\((0,6)\\); (b) \\(x=3\\) or \\(x=1\\)", "markScheme": ["(a) B2 — fully correct V graph with \\((0,6)\\) and \\((1.5,0)\\) marked (B1 for correct shape with vertex on the x-axis)", "(b) M1 — \\(4x-6=2x\\) and \\(4x-6=-2x\\)", "(b) A2 — \\(x=3\\), \\(x=1\\) (A1 for either; alt: \\(12x^2-48x+36=0\\) B1, factorises M1, both answers A1)"]}, {"id": "w25-p12-q6", "group": "mod-eq", "source": {"session": "Oct/Nov 2025", "paper": "4037/12", "question": "6"}, "calc": false, "outcomes": ["4.1"], "topics": ["|quadratic| = d"], "marks": 5, "targetMinutes": 8, "difficulty": 3, "statement": "<p>Solve the equation \\(\\;|2x^2 + x - 10| = 5\\).&emsp;<b>[5]</b></p>", "tips": ["\\(|q(x)| = d\\) (with \\(d>0\\)) splits into <b>two quadratics</b>: \\(q(x) = d\\) and \\(q(x) = -d\\). Solve both completely.", "Expect up to <b>4 solutions</b>. Keep every real root of both quadratics — reject nothing unless it fails the original equation (here nothing fails).", "Non-calculator paper: one quadratic factorises, the other needs the formula — keep the answer in exact surd form.", "Common error: rejecting negative or surd roots \"because of the modulus\". The modulus is on the <i>output</i>, not on \\(x\\) — negative \\(x\\) values are fine."], "solution": ["<p><b>Case 1:</b> \\(2x^2+x-10 = 5 \\Rightarrow 2x^2+x-15 = 0 \\Rightarrow (2x-5)(x+3) = 0 \\Rightarrow x = \\tfrac52,\\; x = -3\\).</p>", "<p><b>Case 2:</b> \\(2x^2+x-10 = -5 \\Rightarrow 2x^2+x-5 = 0\\). This does not factorise; by the quadratic formula \\(x = \\dfrac{-1 \\pm \\sqrt{1+40}}{4} = \\dfrac{-1 \\pm \\sqrt{41}}{4}\\).</p>", "<p>All four values satisfy the original equation (case 1 makes the inside \\(+5\\), case 2 makes it \\(-5\\); the modulus of both is 5).</p>"], "answer": "\\(x = \\tfrac52,\\; -3,\\; \\dfrac{-1+\\sqrt{41}}{4},\\; \\dfrac{-1-\\sqrt{41}}{4}\\)", "markScheme": ["M1 — \\(2x^2+x-15=0\\) with a valid attempt to solve to obtain 2 values", "A1 — \\(x=\\tfrac52\\) and \\(x=-3\\) both (A0 if \\(-3\\) rejected)", "M1 — \\(2x^2+x-5=0\\) (correct 3-term quadratic equated to zero)", "A2 — \\(x=\\dfrac{-1\\pm\\sqrt{41}}{4}\\) both (A0 if the negative root is rejected; final denominator must be positive)"]}, {"id": "w24-p13-q1", "group": "mod-quad-graph", "source": {"session": "Oct/Nov 2024", "paper": "4037/13", "question": "1"}, "calc": false, "outcomes": ["4.1", "4.2"], "alsoChapters": [2], "topics": ["|quadratic| graph", "number of roots of |f(x)|=k"], "marks": 6, "targetMinutes": 9, "difficulty": 2, "parts": [{"label": "(a)", "text": "Find the coordinates of the stationary point on the curve \\(y=(x+3)(x-4)\\).", "marks": 3}, {"label": "(b)", "text": "On the axes, sketch the graph of \\(y=|(x+3)(x-4)|\\), showing the coordinates of the points where the graph meets the axes.", "marks": 2}, {"label": "(c)", "text": "Given that \\(k>0\\), find the values of \\(k\\) for which the equation \\(|(x+3)(x-4)|=k\\) has exactly 2 distinct roots.", "marks": 1}], "statement": "<p><b>(a)</b> Find the coordinates of the stationary point on the curve \\(y=(x+3)(x-4)\\).&emsp;<b>[3]</b></p><p><b>(b)</b> On the axes, sketch the graph of \\(y=|(x+3)(x-4)|\\), showing the coordinates of the points where the graph meets the axes.&emsp;<b>[2]</b></p><p><b>(c)</b> Given that \\(k>0\\), find the values of \\(k\\) for which \\(|(x+3)(x-4)|=k\\) has exactly 2 distinct roots.&emsp;<b>[1]</b></p>", "tips": ["Stationary point of a parabola: by symmetry \\(x\\) is midway between the roots: \\(x=\\frac{-3+4}{2}=\\frac12\\). (Or complete the square, or differentiate.)", "\\(y=|f(x)|\\) for a parabola opening upwards with two roots: fold the part between the roots up — cusps at the roots, a local maximum where the vertex was (its \\(y\\)-value made positive).", "For \"how many roots does \\(|f(x)|=k\\) have?\", draw the horizontal line \\(y=k\\) and count intersections at each height: above the folded hump → 2; through it → 3; across it → 4.", "The folded hump has height \\(\\left|-\\frac{49}{4}\\right| = \\frac{49}{4}\\), so exactly 2 roots when \\(k > \\frac{49}{4}\\)."], "solution": ["<p><b>(a)</b> \\(y = x^2-x-12\\). Symmetry: \\(x=\\tfrac{-3+4}{2}=\\tfrac12\\). Then \\(y = (\\tfrac12+3)(\\tfrac12-4) = \\tfrac72\\times(-\\tfrac72) = -\\tfrac{49}{4}\\). Stationary point \\(\\left(\\tfrac12, -\\tfrac{49}{4}\\right)\\).</p>", "<p><b>(b)</b> Roots at \\(x=-3, 4\\); \\(y\\)-intercept \\(|(3)(-4)| = 12\\). Between the roots the parabola is negative, so it folds up with cusps at \\((-3,0)\\), \\((4,0)\\) and local max \\(\\left(\\tfrac12, \\tfrac{49}{4}\\right)\\).</p>", {"figure": {"x": [-5, 6], "y": [-2, 16], "xTicks": 1, "yTicks": 4, "curves": [{"poly": [1, -1, -12], "abs": true}], "points": [[-3, 0, "(-3, 0)"], [4, 0, "(4, 0)"], [0, 12, "(0, 12)"]]}}, "<p><b>(c)</b> A horizontal line \\(y=k\\) cuts the graph exactly twice when it passes <b>above</b> the folded hump: \\(k > \\tfrac{49}{4}\\). (For \\(0<k<\\tfrac{49}{4}\\) there are 4 roots; at \\(k=\\tfrac{49}{4}\\), 3 roots.)</p>"], "answer": "(a) \\(\\left(\\tfrac12,-\\tfrac{49}{4}\\right)\\); (b) folded parabola, cusps \\((-3,0)\\),\\((4,0)\\), \\(y\\)-int 12; (c) \\(k>\\tfrac{49}{4}\\)", "markScheme": ["(a) M1 — valid method (expand & differentiate / complete square / symmetry); A1 \\(x=\\tfrac12\\); A1 \\(y=-\\tfrac{49}{4}\\)", "(b) B1 — correct shape: parabola with cusps on the x-axis, max in 1st quadrant; B1 — intercepts \\(-3\\), \\(4\\), \\(12\\)", "(c) B1 — \\(k > \\tfrac{49}{4}\\)"]}, {"id": "w25-p23-q3", "group": "mod-quad-graph", "source": {"session": "Oct/Nov 2025", "paper": "4037/23", "question": "3"}, "calc": true, "outcomes": ["4.1", "4.2"], "alsoChapters": [2], "topics": ["completing the square", "|quadratic| graph on grid", "graphical |quadratic| < d"], "marks": 9, "targetMinutes": 14, "difficulty": 3, "parts": [{"label": "(a)(i)", "text": "Write \\(x^2-x-6\\) in the form \\((x+a)^2+b\\), where \\(a\\) and \\(b\\) are constants.", "marks": 2}, {"label": "(a)(ii)", "text": "Hence write down the coordinates of the stationary point on the curve \\(y=x^2-x-6\\).", "marks": 2}, {"label": "(b)", "text": "On the axes, draw the graph of \\(y=|x^2-x-6|\\) for \\(-4 \\leqslant x \\leqslant 4\\).", "marks": 3}, {"label": "(c)", "text": "Use your graph to solve the inequality \\(|x^2-x-6| < 4\\).", "marks": 2}], "statement": "<p><b>(a)(i)</b> Write \\(x^2-x-6\\) in the form \\((x+a)^2+b\\).&emsp;<b>[2]</b></p><p><b>(a)(ii)</b> Hence write down the coordinates of the stationary point on the curve \\(y=x^2-x-6\\).&emsp;<b>[2]</b></p><p><b>(b)</b> On the axes, draw the graph of \\(y=|x^2-x-6|\\) for \\(-4\\leqslant x\\leqslant 4\\).&emsp;<b>[3]</b></p><p><b>(c)</b> Use your graph to solve the inequality \\(|x^2-x-6| < 4\\).&emsp;<b>[2]</b></p>", "tips": ["Completing the square: halve the \\(x\\)-coefficient: \\(x^2-x-6=(x-\\tfrac12)^2-\\tfrac14-6=(x-\\tfrac12)^2-\\tfrac{25}{4}\\). The vertex is at \\((-a, b) = (\\tfrac12, -\\tfrac{25}{4})\\).", "Drawing \\(|x^2-x-6|\\): factorise → \\((x-3)(x+2)\\), roots \\(-2, 3\\). Fold the part between the roots upward; cusps at the roots, folded max \\((\\tfrac12, \\tfrac{25}{4})\\), \\(y\\)-intercept 6. Plot endpoints too: \\(x=-4 \\Rightarrow y=14\\); \\(x=4 \\Rightarrow y=6\\).", "\"Use your graph\" = draw \\(y=4\\) and read the \\(x\\)-values of the intersections (to about 1 d.p.), then write the intervals where the curve is <b>below</b> the line.", "\\(|f(x)| < 4\\) has TWO separate intervals here — one around each cusp (the curve dips below 4 near each root)."], "solution": ["<p><b>(a)(i)</b> \\(x^2-x-6=\\left(x-\\tfrac12\\right)^2-\\tfrac14-6=\\left(x-\\tfrac12\\right)^2-\\tfrac{25}{4}\\), so \\(a=-\\tfrac12,\\; b=-\\tfrac{25}{4}\\).</p>", "<p><b>(a)(ii)</b> Stationary (minimum) point at \\(\\left(\\tfrac12, -\\tfrac{25}{4}\\right)\\).</p>", "<p><b>(b)</b> \\(x^2-x-6=(x-3)(x+2)\\): roots \\(-2\\) and \\(3\\). Fold the negative arc up: cusps \\((-2,0)\\), \\((3,0)\\); folded maximum \\(\\left(\\tfrac12,\\tfrac{25}{4}\\right)\\); \\(y\\)-intercept \\((0,6)\\); ends \\((-4,14)\\) and \\((4,6)\\).</p>", {"figure": {"x": [-4, 4], "y": [0, 15], "xTicks": 1, "yTicks": 2, "grid": true, "curves": [{"poly": [1, -1, -6], "abs": true}], "hlines": [4], "points": [[-2, 0, "(-2,0)"], [3, 0, "(3,0)"], [0, 6, "(0,6)"]]}}, "<p><b>(c)</b> Draw \\(y=4\\). It meets the curve where \\(x^2-x-6=\\pm 4\\):<br>\\(x^2-x-2=0 \\Rightarrow (x-2)(x+1)=0 \\Rightarrow x=-1, 2\\) (inner crossings, on the folded arch);<br>\\(x^2-x-10=0 \\Rightarrow x=\\frac{1\\pm\\sqrt{41}}{2} \\approx -2.7,\\; 3.7\\) (outer crossings).</p>", "<p>The curve is <b>below</b> \\(y=4\\) on two intervals: \\(-2.7 < x < -1\\) and \\(2 < x < 3.7\\) (graph reading to 1 d.p.).</p>"], "answer": "(a)(i) \\((x-\\tfrac12)^2-\\tfrac{25}{4}\\); (ii) \\((\\tfrac12,-\\tfrac{25}{4})\\); (c) \\(-2.7<x<-1\\) and \\(2<x<3.7\\)", "markScheme": ["(a)(i) B2 — \\((x-\\tfrac12)^2-\\tfrac{25}{4}\\) (B1 for \\((x-\\tfrac12)^2\\) seen or both values stated)", "(a)(ii) B2 — \\((\\tfrac12, -\\tfrac{25}{4})\\) strict FT from (i)", "(b) B3 — correct shape with cusps at \\((-2,0)\\), \\((3,0)\\), intercept \\((0,6)\\), max in 1st quadrant (B1 shape with cusps; B1 intercepts)", "(c) B2 — \\(-2.7 < x < -1\\) and \\(2 < x < 3.7\\) (their graph-read outer values; B1 for either interval)"]}, {"id": "s25-p22-q1", "group": "mod-ineq", "source": {"session": "May/June 2025", "paper": "4037/22", "question": "1"}, "calc": true, "outcomes": ["4.2"], "topics": ["|ax+b| ⩾ c"], "marks": 4, "targetMinutes": 6, "difficulty": 1, "statement": "<p>Solve the inequality \\(\\;|5x+2| \\geqslant 3\\).&emsp;<b>[4]</b></p>", "tips": ["\\(|A| \\geqslant c\\) means \\(A\\) is at distance \\(\\geqslant c\\) from 0: \\(A \\geqslant c\\) <b>or</b> \\(A \\leqslant -c\\). Two separate intervals — connect them with \"or\", never \"and\".", "(Contrast: \\(|A| \\leqslant c\\) gives ONE interval \\(-c \\leqslant A \\leqslant c\\).)", "Keep the inequality signs: solve each linear inequality exactly as an equation but carrying the sign through."], "solution": ["<p>\\(|5x+2|\\geqslant 3\\) means \\(5x+2 \\geqslant 3\\) or \\(5x+2 \\leqslant -3\\).</p>", "<p>First: \\(5x \\geqslant 1 \\Rightarrow x \\geqslant \\tfrac15\\).&emsp;Second: \\(5x \\leqslant -5 \\Rightarrow x \\leqslant -1\\).</p>", "<p>Sketch check: the V of \\(y=|5x+2|\\) is at or above the line \\(y=3\\) outside the two crossing points \\(x=-1\\) and \\(x=\\tfrac15\\).</p>"], "answer": "\\(x \\leqslant -1\\) or \\(x \\geqslant \\tfrac15\\)", "markScheme": ["M1 — \\(5x+2 * 3\\) and \\(5x+2 * -3\\) oe, where * is any inequality sign or = (or squares: \\((5x+2)^2 * 3^2\\))", "A2 — critical values \\(\\tfrac15\\) and \\(-1\\) soi (A1 for one correct value)", "A1 — \\(x \\leqslant -1\\), \\(x \\geqslant \\tfrac15\\) oe — mark the final answer"]}, {"id": "s26-p22-q4", "group": "mod-quad-graph", "source": {"session": "May/June 2026", "paper": "4037/22", "question": "4"}, "calc": true, "outcomes": ["4.1"], "topics": ["|quadratic| sketch", "reverse-engineer f from |f(x)|=4"], "marks": 7, "targetMinutes": 11, "difficulty": 3, "parts": [{"label": "(a)", "text": "On the axes, sketch the graph of \\(y = |x^2-5|\\). State the intercepts with the coordinate axes.", "marks": 3}, {"label": "(b)", "text": "It is given that \\(f(x) = ax+b\\), where \\(a\\) and \\(b\\) are non-zero integers. The solutions of the equation \\(|f(x)| = 4\\) are \\(x = 2.5\\) and \\(x = -1.5\\). Find the possible expressions for \\(f(x)\\).", "marks": 4}], "statement": "<p><b>(a)</b> On the axes, sketch the graph of \\(\\;y = |x^2-5|\\). State the intercepts with the coordinate axes.&emsp;<b>[3]</b></p><p><b>(b)</b> It is given that \\(f(x) = ax+b\\), where \\(a\\) and \\(b\\) are non-zero integers. The solutions of the equation \\(\\;|f(x)| = 4\\) are \\(x = 2.5\\) and \\(x = -1.5\\).<br>Find the possible expressions for \\(f(x)\\).&emsp;<b>[4]</b></p>", "tips": ["(a) \\(x^2-5\\) dips below the axis between the roots \\(\\pm\\sqrt5\\) — fold that arch up: cusps at \\((\\pm\\sqrt5, 0)\\), local max \\((0, 5)\\) (the y-intercept made positive).", "(b) A linear function hits each value once — so one solution comes from \\(f(x)=4\\) and the other from \\(f(x)=-4\\) (they cannot both give the same value).", "Two pairings → two equations each: \\(2.5a+b=4,\\ -1.5a+b=-4\\) (gives \\(a=2, b=-1\\)) or the signs swapped (gives \\(a=-2, b=1\\)).", "Answer BOTH expressions: \\(f(x) = 2x-1\\) and \\(f(x) = -2x+1\\) — and note they are negatives of each other (same modulus!)."], "solution": ["<p><b>(a)</b> Roots of \\(x^2-5\\): \\(x=\\pm\\sqrt5\\); \\(y\\)-intercept \\(|{-5}| = 5\\). Fold the arch between the roots up: cusps at \\(\\pm\\sqrt5\\), local maximum \\((0,5)\\), outer branches rising.</p>", {"figure": {"x": [-4, 4], "y": [-1, 8], "xTicks": 1, "yTicks": 1, "curves": [{"poly": [1, 0, -5], "abs": true}], "points": [[-2.2361, 0, "−√5"], [2.2361, 0, "√5"], [0, 5, "(0, 5)"]]}}, "<p><b>(b)</b> Since f is linear, the two solutions must give opposite signs of f:</p>", "<p>Case 1: \\(f(2.5) = 4\\) and \\(f(-1.5) = -4\\): subtracting, \\(4a = 8 \\Rightarrow a = 2\\), then \\(b = 4 - 5 = -1\\): \\(f(x) = 2x-1\\).</p>", "<p>Case 2: \\(f(2.5) = -4\\) and \\(f(-1.5) = 4\\): \\(a = -2\\), \\(b = 1\\): \\(f(x) = -2x+1\\).</p>", "<p>Check: \\(|2(2.5)-1| = 4\\;✓\\), \\(|2(-1.5)-1| = |-4| = 4\\;✓\\). Both expressions valid (each is the negative of the other).</p>"], "answer": "(a) cusps \\((\\pm\\sqrt5,0)\\), max \\((0,5)\\); (b) \\(f(x) = 2x-1\\) and \\(f(x) = -2x+1\\)", "markScheme": ["(a) B2 — correct smooth curve: two cusps on the x-axis equidistant from O, local max on the y-axis, correct curvatures (B1 for three of the four properties); B1 — intercepts 5, \\(-\\sqrt5\\), \\(\\sqrt5\\) indicated", "(b) B1 — a valid pair of linear equations, e.g. \\(2.5a+b=4\\) and \\(-1.5a+b=-4\\) oe", "(b) M1 — eliminates one unknown from two valid equations; A1 — \\(a=2, b=-1\\) or \\(a=-2, b=1\\) soi", "(b) A1 — \\(2x-1\\) AND \\(-2x+1\\), and no others"]}, {"id": "w25-p13-q1b", "group": "mod-ineq", "source": {"session": "Oct/Nov 2025", "paper": "4037/13", "question": "1(b)"}, "calc": false, "outcomes": ["4.2"], "topics": ["|ax+b| < cx+d"], "marks": 4, "targetMinutes": 6, "difficulty": 2, "statement": "<p>Solve the inequality \\(\\;|3x-4| < x+2\\).&emsp;<b>[4]</b></p>", "tips": ["With a variable on the right, split into the two boundary equations/inequalities: \\(3x-4 < x+2\\) <b>and</b> \\(-(3x-4) < x+2\\) (i.e. \\(3x-4 > -x-2\\)). The solution set satisfies <b>both</b> → one interval between the critical values.", "Cleanest method: <b>square both sides</b> (valid because both sides must be \\(\\geqslant 0\\) where solutions exist): \\(9x^2-24x+16 < x^2+4x+4 \\Rightarrow (2x-1)(x-3) < 0 \\Rightarrow \\tfrac12 < x < 3\\).", "Give the answer as a single sandwich \\(\\tfrac12 < x < 3\\); listing \\(x>\\tfrac12\\), \\(x<3\\) separately without \"and\" loses the final mark."], "solution": ["<p><b>Squaring method:</b> both sides of \\(|3x-4| < x+2\\): \\(9x^2-24x+16 < x^2+4x+4\\Rightarrow 8x^2-28x+12<0 \\Rightarrow 2x^2-7x+3<0 \\Rightarrow (2x-1)(x-3)<0\\).</p>", "<p>Critical values \\(\\tfrac12\\) and \\(3\\); a positive quadratic is negative <b>between</b> its roots: \\(\\tfrac12 < x < 3\\).</p>", "<p><i>Case method:</i> \\(3x-4 < x+2 \\Rightarrow x < 3\\); and \\(3x-4 > -(x+2) \\Rightarrow 4x > 2 \\Rightarrow x > \\tfrac12\\). Both must hold: \\(\\tfrac12 < x < 3\\).</p>"], "answer": "\\(\\tfrac12 < x < 3\\)", "markScheme": ["B1 — critical value \\(x = 3\\)", "M1 — \\(-3x+4 < x+2\\) oe (second case)", "A1 — critical value \\(x = \\tfrac12\\)", "A1 — \\(\\tfrac12 < x < 3\\) (must be combined with \"and\"/sandwich form)"]}, {"id": "w24-p22-q4", "group": "mod-ineq", "source": {"session": "Oct/Nov 2024", "paper": "4037/22", "question": "4"}, "calc": true, "outcomes": ["4.2"], "topics": ["graphical method", "|ax+b| > c"], "marks": 5, "targetMinutes": 8, "difficulty": 2, "statement": "<p>Use a <b>graphical method</b> to solve the inequality \\(\\;|2x-8| > 4\\).&emsp;<b>[5]</b></p><p class=\"note\">A grid is provided (\\(x\\) from \\(-2\\) to \\(8\\), \\(y\\) from \\(0\\) to \\(10\\)).</p>", "tips": ["\"Graphical method\" is <b>required</b> here: you must draw \\(y=|2x-8|\\) AND the horizontal line \\(y=4\\) — marks are for the graphs.", "\\(y=|2x-8|\\): V with vertex where \\(2x-8=0\\Rightarrow (4,0)\\), passing through \\((0,8)\\).", "Read the crossing points: \\(|2x-8|=4\\) at \\(x=2\\) and \\(x=6\\). The V is <b>above</b> the line outside them.", "Strict inequality → strict answer: \\(x<2\\) or \\(x>6\\)."], "solution": ["<p>Draw \\(y=|2x-8|\\): vertex \\((4,0)\\), through \\((0,8)\\) and \\((8,8)\\). Draw the line \\(y=4\\).</p>", {"figure": {"x": [-2, 8], "y": [0, 10], "xTicks": 1, "yTicks": 1, "grid": true, "curves": [{"poly": [2, -8], "abs": true}], "hlines": [4], "points": [[2, 4, "x=2"], [6, 4, "x=6"]]}}, "<p>Intersections: \\(2x-8=4\\Rightarrow x=6\\) and \\(2x-8=-4\\Rightarrow x=2\\).</p>", "<p>The V-graph is above \\(y=4\\) to the left of \\(x=2\\) and to the right of \\(x=6\\): answer \\(x<2\\) or \\(x>6\\).</p>"], "answer": "\\(x < 2\\) or \\(x > 6\\)", "markScheme": ["M1 — attempt to draw \\(y=|2x-8|\\) (sections \\((0,8)\\)–\\((2,4)\\) and \\((6,4)\\)–\\((8,8)\\), or V with vertex \\((4,0)\\))", "A1 — correct graph;  B1 — \\(y=4\\) drawn", "M1 — critical values 2 and 6 (dep on graphs);  A1 — \\(x<2\\), \\(x>6\\)"]}, {"id": "s25-p11-q4a", "group": "subst", "source": {"session": "May/June 2025", "paper": "4037/11", "question": "4(a)"}, "calc": false, "outcomes": ["4.3"], "topics": ["fractional powers", "reject invalid root"], "marks": 4, "targetMinutes": 6, "difficulty": 2, "statement": "<p>Solve the equation \\(\\;x^{\\frac13} - x^{\\frac16} = 2\\).&emsp;<b>[4]</b></p>", "tips": ["Spot the disguise: \\(x^{1/3} = \\left(x^{1/6}\\right)^2\\). Substitute \\(u = x^{1/6}\\) to get \\(u^2 - u - 2 = 0\\).", "\\(x^{1/6}\\) is an even-type root — it cannot be negative. After solving for \\(u\\), <b>reject</b> \\(u=-1\\); the rejection earns a mark (B1).", "Undo the substitution with the 6th power: \\(u=2 \\Rightarrow x = 2^6 = 64\\).", "Contrast with cube-root equations, where negative values ARE allowed."], "solution": ["<p>Let \\(u = x^{1/6}\\), so \\(x^{1/3} = u^2\\): \\(u^2 - u - 2 = 0 \\Rightarrow (u-2)(u+1) = 0 \\Rightarrow u = 2\\) or \\(u = -1\\).</p>", "<p>\\(u = x^{1/6} \\geqslant 0\\), so \\(u = -1\\) is <b>rejected</b>. (If you force \\(x = (-1)^6 = 1\\): check \\(1 - 1 = 0 \\neq 2\\) — it fails the original equation.)</p>", "<p>\\(u = 2 \\Rightarrow x = 2^6 = 64\\). Check: \\(64^{1/3} - 64^{1/6} = 4 - 2 = 2\\) ✓</p>"], "answer": "\\(x = 64\\) only", "markScheme": ["M1 — solves/factorises \\(x^{1/3}-x^{1/6}-2=0\\) as a quadratic, e.g. \\((x^{1/6}+1)(x^{1/6}-2)=0\\)", "A1 — \\(x^{1/6}=2\\) (and \\(x^{1/6}=-1\\) seen)", "A1 — \\(x=64\\)", "B1 — rejects \\(x^{1/6}=-1\\) (or rejects \\(x=1\\))"]}, {"id": "s25-p12-q2", "group": "subst", "source": {"session": "May/June 2025", "paper": "4037/12", "question": "2"}, "calc": false, "outcomes": ["4.3"], "topics": ["cube roots", "keep negative root"], "marks": 4, "targetMinutes": 6, "difficulty": 2, "statement": "<p>Solve the equation \\(\\;x^{\\frac13} + 1 = \\dfrac{6}{x^{\\frac13}}\\).&emsp;<b>[4]</b></p>", "tips": ["Multiply through by \\(x^{1/3}\\) first: \\(x^{2/3} + x^{1/3} - 6 = 0\\) — a quadratic in \\(u = x^{1/3}\\).", "Cube roots CAN be negative: \\(u = -3\\) is valid and gives \\(x = (-3)^3 = -27\\). <b>Do not reject it</b> — the mark scheme gives A0 if you do!", "Compare with \\(x^{1/6}\\)-type equations where the negative branch must be rejected. The parity of the root decides."], "solution": ["<p>Multiply by \\(x^{1/3}\\) (\\(x \\neq 0\\)): \\(x^{2/3} + x^{1/3} - 6 = 0\\). Let \\(u = x^{1/3}\\): \\(u^2 + u - 6 = 0 \\Rightarrow (u+3)(u-2) = 0\\).</p>", "<p>\\(u = 2 \\Rightarrow x = 2^3 = 8\\). Check: \\(2 + 1 = 3 = 6/2\\) ✓</p>", "<p>\\(u = -3 \\Rightarrow x = (-3)^3 = -27\\). Check: \\((-27)^{1/3}+1 = -3+1 = -2\\) and \\(6/(-3) = -2\\) ✓ valid!</p>"], "answer": "\\(x = 8\\) and \\(x = -27\\) (both valid)", "markScheme": ["B1 — \\(x^{2/3}+x^{1/3}-6 = 0\\) oe (multiplying through by \\(x^{1/3}\\); allow with substitution)", "M1 — factorises, e.g. \\((x^{1/3}+3)(x^{1/3}-2)=0\\)", "A1 — \\(x=-27\\) (A0 if \\(-27\\) is rejected)", "A1 — \\(x=8\\)"]}, {"id": "s24-p22-q3b", "group": "subst", "source": {"session": "May/June 2024", "paper": "4037/22", "question": "3(b)"}, "calc": true, "outcomes": ["4.3"], "topics": ["cube roots", "reciprocal form"], "marks": 4, "targetMinutes": 6, "difficulty": 2, "statement": "<p>Solve the equation \\(\\;\\dfrac{12}{\\sqrt[3]{x}} - \\sqrt[3]{x} = 4\\).&emsp;<b>[4]</b></p>", "tips": ["Multiply through by \\(\\sqrt[3]{x}\\) to clear the fraction: \\(12 - (\\sqrt[3]{x})^2 = 4\\sqrt[3]{x}\\), i.e. \\(u^2+4u-12=0\\) with \\(u=\\sqrt[3]{x}\\).", "Cube roots take negative values — keep \\(u=-6\\) and cube it: \\(x = -216\\).", "Cube at the END: \\(x = u^3\\). Cubing preserves sign, so both answers survive."], "solution": ["<p>Let \\(u = \\sqrt[3]{x}\\). Multiply by \\(u\\): \\(12 - u^2 = 4u \\Rightarrow u^2 + 4u - 12 = 0 \\Rightarrow (u+6)(u-2) = 0\\).</p>", "<p>\\(u = 2 \\Rightarrow x = 8\\). Check: \\(12/2 - 2 = 4\\) ✓</p>", "<p>\\(u = -6 \\Rightarrow x = (-6)^3 = -216\\). Check: \\(12/(-6) - (-6) = -2 + 6 = 4\\) ✓</p>"], "answer": "\\(x = 8\\) and \\(x = -216\\)", "markScheme": ["B1 — \\((\\sqrt[3]{x})^2 + 4\\sqrt[3]{x} - 12 = 0\\) soi (or \\(y=\\sqrt[3]{x}\\), \\(y^2+4y-12=0\\))", "M1 — factorises or solves their 3-term quadratic in \\(\\sqrt[3]{x}\\)", "A2 — \\(x=8\\) and \\(x=-216\\) (A1 for \\(\\sqrt[3]{x}=2\\), \\(\\sqrt[3]{x}=-6\\))"]}, {"id": "s24-p11-q9b", "group": "subst", "source": {"session": "May/June 2024", "paper": "4037/11", "question": "9(b)"}, "calc": false, "outcomes": ["4.3"], "alsoChapters": [6], "topics": ["exponential equations", "reciprocal", "exact form"], "marks": 4, "targetMinutes": 6, "difficulty": 3, "statement": "<p>Solve the equation \\(\\;10e^{2x-1} - 11 = 6e^{1-2x}\\), giving your answer in exact form.&emsp;<b>[4]</b></p>", "tips": ["Key spot: \\(e^{1-2x} = \\dfrac{1}{e^{2x-1}}\\) (the exponents are negatives of each other).", "Let \\(u = e^{2x-1}\\): \\(10u - 11 = \\dfrac{6}{u} \\Rightarrow 10u^2 - 11u - 6 = 0\\).", "An exponential is always positive: reject the negative root \\(u=-\\tfrac25\\) — say so explicitly (A0 if you don't discount it).", "\"Exact form\" = leave as logarithms: \\(2x-1 = \\ln\\tfrac32 \\Rightarrow x = \\tfrac12 + \\tfrac12\\ln\\tfrac32\\). No decimals."], "solution": ["<p>Since \\(e^{1-2x} = \\dfrac{1}{e^{2x-1}}\\), put \\(u = e^{2x-1}\\): \\(10u - 11 = \\dfrac6u \\Rightarrow 10u^2 - 11u - 6 = 0\\).</p>", "<p>Factorise: \\((5u+2)(2u-3) = 0 \\Rightarrow u = -\\tfrac25\\) or \\(u = \\tfrac32\\). But \\(u = e^{2x-1} > 0\\), so <b>reject</b> \\(u=-\\tfrac25\\).</p>", "<p>\\(e^{2x-1} = \\tfrac32 \\Rightarrow 2x - 1 = \\ln\\tfrac32 \\Rightarrow x = \\tfrac12 + \\tfrac12\\ln\\tfrac32\\).</p>"], "answer": "\\(x = \\tfrac12 + \\tfrac12\\ln\\tfrac32\\) (exact equivalents accepted)", "markScheme": ["B1 — correct use of the reciprocal: \\(e^{1-2x} = 1/e^{2x-1}\\)", "M1 — forms a quadratic in \\(e^{2x-1}\\): \\(10(e^{2x-1})^2 - 11e^{2x-1} - 6 = 0\\)", "M1 — solves their quadratic (dep)", "A1 — \\(x = \\tfrac12+\\tfrac12\\ln\\tfrac32\\) oe exact only; A0 if the negative root is not discounted"]}, {"id": "w24-p22-q5b", "group": "subst", "source": {"session": "Oct/Nov 2024", "paper": "4037/22", "question": "5(b)"}, "calc": true, "outcomes": ["4.3"], "alsoChapters": [6], "topics": ["exponential equations", "reciprocal"], "marks": 4, "targetMinutes": 6, "difficulty": 3, "statement": "<p>Solve the equation \\(\\;e^{2x+1} - 10e^{-2x-1} = 3\\).&emsp;<b>[4]</b></p>", "tips": ["\\(e^{-2x-1} = \\dfrac{1}{e^{2x+1}}\\). Multiply through by \\(e^{2x+1}\\) to get a quadratic in \\(u = e^{2x+1}\\): \\(u^2 - 3u - 10 = 0\\).", "Reject the negative root (\\(u=-2\\)): exponentials are positive. State it — \"and no other solution\" is part of the final mark.", "Finish with \\(\\ln\\): \\(2x+1 = \\ln 5 \\Rightarrow x = \\frac{\\ln 5 - 1}{2}\\) (calculator paper: 0.305 also accepted)."], "solution": ["<p>Multiply by \\(e^{2x+1}\\): \\(\\left(e^{2x+1}\\right)^2 - 3e^{2x+1} - 10 = 0\\).</p>", "<p>Factorise with \\(u=e^{2x+1}\\): \\((u-5)(u+2)=0 \\Rightarrow u = 5\\) (reject \\(u=-2<0\\)).</p>", "<p>\\(e^{2x+1} = 5 \\Rightarrow 2x+1 = \\ln 5 \\Rightarrow x = \\dfrac{\\ln 5 - 1}{2} \\approx 0.305\\).</p>"], "answer": "\\(x = \\dfrac{\\ln 5 - 1}{2} \\approx 0.305\\), no other solution", "markScheme": ["B1 — \\(e^{4x+2} - 3e^{2x+1} - 10 = 0\\) oe (quadratic in \\(e^{2x+1}\\))", "M1 — solves/factorises their 3-term quadratic in \\(e^{2x+1}\\)", "A1 — \\(e^{2x+1} = 5\\) nfww", "A1 — \\(x = \\frac{-1+\\ln 5}{2}\\) oe or 0.305, and no other solution"]}, {"id": "s26-p12-q3", "group": "subst", "source": {"session": "May/June 2026", "paper": "4037/12", "question": "3"}, "calc": false, "outcomes": ["4.3"], "alsoChapters": [3], "topics": ["factor theorem", "substitution u²", "hence"], "marks": 7, "targetMinutes": 11, "difficulty": 3, "parts": [{"label": "(a)", "text": "The polynomial p is given by \\(p(x) = 6x^3 - x^2 - 5x + 2\\). Find the remainder when \\(p(x)\\) is divided by \\(x+2\\).", "marks": 1}, {"label": "(b)", "text": "Write \\(p(x)\\) as a product of linear factors.", "marks": 4}, {"label": "(c)", "text": "Hence solve the equation \\(6u^6 - u^4 - 5u^2 + 2 = 0\\).", "marks": 2}], "statement": "<p>The polynomial p is given by \\(\\;p(x) = 6x^3 - x^2 - 5x + 2\\).</p><p><b>(a)</b> Find the remainder when \\(p(x)\\) is divided by \\(x+2\\).&emsp;<b>[1]</b></p><p><b>(b)</b> Write \\(p(x)\\) as a product of linear factors.&emsp;<b>[4]</b></p><p><b>(c)</b> Hence solve the equation \\(\\;6u^6 - u^4 - 5u^2 + 2 = 0\\).&emsp;<b>[2]</b></p>", "tips": ["(a) Remainder theorem: remainder on division by \\(x+2\\) is \\(p(-2)\\) — no long division needed.", "(b) Trial small values for a root: \\(p(-1) = -6-1+5+2 = 0\\), so \\(x+1\\) is a factor. Divide to get the quadratic factor, then factorise it.", "(c) \"Hence\" — the sextic is \\(p(u^2)\\): every linear factor \\(px - q\\) of p becomes \\(pu^2 - q\\). Solve \\(u^2 = \\text{each root of } p\\).", "\\(u^2\\) must be \\(\\geqslant 0\\): the root \\(x = -1\\) gives \\(u^2 = -1\\) — no real solutions from that factor. The other two roots each give a ± pair."], "solution": ["<p><b>(a)</b> \\(p(-2) = 6(-8) - 4 + 10 + 2 = -48 - 4 + 10 + 2 = -40\\).</p>", "<p><b>(b)</b> \\(p(-1) = 0\\), so \\((x+1)\\) is a factor. Division: \\(p(x) = (x+1)(6x^2 - 7x + 2)\\). Factorise the quadratic: \\(6x^2-7x+2 = (3x-2)(2x-1)\\). So \\(p(x) = (x+1)(3x-2)(2x-1)\\).</p>", "<p><b>(c)</b> Substitute \\(x = u^2\\): \\(6u^6 - u^4 - 5u^2 + 2 = (u^2+1)(3u^2-2)(2u^2-1) = 0\\).</p>", "<p>\\(u^2 = -1\\): no real solutions. \\(u^2 = \\tfrac23 \\Rightarrow u = \\pm\\sqrt{\\tfrac23}\\). \\(u^2 = \\tfrac12 \\Rightarrow u = \\pm\\sqrt{\\tfrac12}\\).</p>"], "answer": "(a) \\(-40\\); (b) \\((x+1)(3x-2)(2x-1)\\); (c) \\(u = \\pm\\sqrt{\\tfrac23},\\; \\pm\\sqrt{\\tfrac12}\\)", "markScheme": ["(a) B1 — \\(-40\\)", "(b) M1 — finds a correct linear factor; M1 dep — quadratic factor with any two terms correct; A1 — correct quadratic factor; A1 — \\((x+1)(3x-2)(2x-1)\\)", "(c) B1FT — any two solutions from factors \\((px-q)\\) with \\(pq>0\\); B1 — all four: \\(u=\\pm\\sqrt{2/3},\\ \\pm\\sqrt{1/2}\\) oe (condone excess from \\(u^2+1=0\\) rejected)"]}, {"id": "s24-p11-q1", "group": "cubic", "source": {"session": "May/June 2024", "paper": "4037/11", "question": "1"}, "calc": false, "outcomes": ["4.4", "4.5"], "topics": ["negative cubic sketch", "cubic inequality ⩾ 0"], "marks": 5, "targetMinutes": 8, "difficulty": 2, "parts": [{"label": "(a)", "text": "On the axes, sketch the graph of \\(y = -\\tfrac15(x+2)(2x-1)(x+5)\\), stating the intercepts with the coordinate axes.", "marks": 3}, {"label": "(b)", "text": "Hence solve the inequality \\(-\\tfrac15(x+2)(2x-1)(x+5) \\geqslant 0\\).", "marks": 2}], "statement": "<p><b>(a)</b> On the axes, sketch the graph of \\(\\;y = -\\tfrac15(x+2)(2x-1)(x+5)\\), stating the intercepts with the coordinate axes.&emsp;<b>[3]</b></p><p><b>(b)</b> Hence solve the inequality \\(\\;-\\tfrac15(x+2)(2x-1)(x+5) \\geqslant 0\\).&emsp;<b>[2]</b></p>", "tips": ["Roots from the factors: \\(x = -2,\\ \\tfrac12,\\ -5\\). \\(y\\)-intercept: substitute \\(x=0\\): \\(-\\tfrac15(2)(-1)(5) = 2\\).", "Shape: the \\(x^3\\) coefficient is negative (\\(-\\tfrac15\\cdot 2 = -\\tfrac25\\)) → graph falls to the right (down-up-down through the three roots).", "For \\(y \\geqslant 0\\), read the sketch: the intervals where the curve is on or above the axis. A negative cubic with roots \\(a<b<c\\) is \\(\\geqslant 0\\) on \\(x \\leqslant a\\) and \\(b \\leqslant x \\leqslant c\\).", "Inequality is non-strict (\\(\\geqslant\\)) → use \\(\\leqslant/\\geqslant\\) in the answer (include the roots)."], "solution": ["<p><b>(a)</b> Roots: \\(-5, -2, \\tfrac12\\). \\(y\\)-intercept 2. Negative leading coefficient → the curve comes from \\(+\\infty\\), crosses \\(-5\\) (down), turns, crosses \\(-2\\) (up), crosses \\(\\tfrac12\\) (down) and heads to \\(-\\infty\\).</p>", {"figure": {"x": [-6.5, 2.5], "y": [-8, 8], "xTicks": 1, "yTicks": 2, "curves": [{"poly": [-0.4, -2.6, -2.6, 2]}], "points": [[-5, 0, "-5"], [-2, 0, "-2"], [0.5, 0, "½"], [0, 2, "(0, 2)"]]}}, "<p><b>(b)</b> \\(y \\geqslant 0\\) where the curve is on/above the \\(x\\)-axis: \\(x \\leqslant -5\\) and \\(-2 \\leqslant x \\leqslant \\tfrac12\\).</p>"], "answer": "(b) \\(x \\leqslant -5\\) or \\(-2 \\leqslant x \\leqslant \\tfrac12\\)", "markScheme": ["(a) B1 — cubic shape with max/min in correct quadrants; B1 — \\(-5, -2, \\tfrac12\\) on the x-axis; B1 — 2 on the y-axis", "(b) B1 — \\(x \\leqslant -5\\); B1 — \\(-2 \\leqslant x \\leqslant \\tfrac12\\) (SC1 for both strict)"]}, {"id": "s24-p22-q1", "group": "cubic", "source": {"session": "May/June 2024", "paper": "4037/22", "question": "1"}, "calc": true, "outcomes": ["4.4", "4.5"], "topics": ["cubic sketch", "cubic inequality ⩽ 0", "modulus of cubic"], "marks": 6, "targetMinutes": 9, "difficulty": 2, "parts": [{"label": "(a)", "text": "On the axes, sketch the graph of \\(y = (2x-5)(x+3)(1-x)\\), stating the intercepts with the coordinate axes.", "marks": 3}, {"label": "(b)(i)", "text": "Hence solve the inequality \\((2x-5)(x+3)(1-x) \\leqslant 0\\).", "marks": 2}, {"label": "(b)(ii)", "text": "On a second set of axes, sketch the graph of \\(y = |(2x-5)(x+3)(1-x)|\\).", "marks": 1}], "statement": "<p><b>(a)</b> On the axes, sketch the graph of \\(\\;y = (2x-5)(x+3)(1-x)\\), stating the intercepts with the coordinate axes.&emsp;<b>[3]</b></p><p><b>(b)(i)</b> Hence solve the inequality \\(\\;(2x-5)(x+3)(1-x) \\leqslant 0\\).&emsp;<b>[2]</b></p><p><b>(b)(ii)</b> On a second set of axes, sketch the graph of \\(\\;y = |(2x-5)(x+3)(1-x)|\\).&emsp;<b>[1]</b></p>", "tips": ["Careful with the factor \\((1-x)\\): root \\(x=1\\), and it contributes a NEGATIVE \\(x\\)-coefficient — overall leading coefficient \\(2\\cdot1\\cdot(-1) = -2\\) (negative cubic).", "\\(y\\)-intercept: \\((0-5)(0+3)(1-0) = -15\\).", "For the modulus sketch: reflect every part below the axis upward — cusps appear AT the roots; the y-intercept becomes \\(+15\\).", "The ends of a modulus graph never go below the axis — both ends rise."], "solution": ["<p><b>(a)</b> Roots: \\(\\tfrac52, -3, 1\\). \\(y\\)-intercept \\(-15\\). Leading coefficient \\(-2\\) → falls to the right.</p>", {"figure": {"x": [-4.5, 4], "y": [-30, 30], "xTicks": 1, "yTicks": 10, "curves": [{"poly": [-2, 1, 16, -15]}], "points": [[-3, 0, "-3"], [1, 0, "1"], [2.5, 0, "2.5"], [0, -15, "(0, -15)"]]}}, "<p><b>(b)(i)</b> \\(y \\leqslant 0\\) where the curve is on/below the axis: \\(-3 \\leqslant x \\leqslant 1\\) and \\(x \\geqslant \\tfrac52\\).</p>", "<p><b>(b)(ii)</b> Fold the negative parts up — cusps at \\(-3, 1, \\tfrac52\\); \\(y\\)-intercept \\((0, 15)\\); both ends rise.</p>", {"figure": {"x": [-4.5, 4], "y": [0, 32], "xTicks": 1, "yTicks": 10, "curves": [{"poly": [-2, 1, 16, -15], "abs": true}], "points": [[0, 15, "(0, 15)"]]}}], "answer": "(b)(i) \\(-3 \\leqslant x \\leqslant 1\\) or \\(x \\geqslant \\tfrac52\\)", "markScheme": ["(a) B1 — correct shape, ends extending above and below the axis; B1 — roots \\(-3, 1, 2.5\\); B1 — y-intercept \\(-15\\)", "(b)(i) B2 — \\(-3 \\leqslant x \\leqslant 1\\), \\(x \\geqslant 2.5\\) (B1 one correct; SC1 both strict)", "(b)(ii) B1 — correct folded shape with cusps, positive y-intercept, intercepts matching (a)"]}, {"id": "w25-p12-q1", "group": "cubic", "source": {"session": "Oct/Nov 2025", "paper": "4037/12", "question": "1"}, "calc": false, "outcomes": ["4.4", "4.5"], "topics": ["negative cubic sketch", "cubic inequality ⩽ 0"], "marks": 5, "targetMinutes": 8, "difficulty": 2, "parts": [{"label": "(a)", "text": "On the axes, sketch the graph of \\(y = (1-x)(x-5)(2x-5)\\), stating the intercepts with the coordinate axes.", "marks": 3}, {"label": "(b)", "text": "Hence solve the inequality \\((1-x)(x-5)(2x-5) \\leqslant 0\\).", "marks": 2}], "statement": "<p><b>(a)</b> On the axes, sketch the graph of \\(\\;y = (1-x)(x-5)(2x-5)\\), stating the intercepts with the coordinate axes.&emsp;<b>[3]</b></p><p><b>(b)</b> Hence solve the inequality \\(\\;(1-x)(x-5)(2x-5) \\leqslant 0\\).&emsp;<b>[2]</b></p>", "tips": ["Roots: \\(1, 5, \\tfrac52\\). \\(y\\)-intercept: \\((1)(-5)(-5) = 25\\).", "Leading coefficient: \\((-1)\\cdot 1 \\cdot 2 = -2\\) → negative cubic (falls to the right).", "Negative cubic with roots \\(1 < \\tfrac52 < 5\\): the curve is \\(\\leqslant 0\\) between the first two roots and after the last one."], "solution": ["<p><b>(a)</b> Roots \\(1, \\tfrac52, 5\\); \\(y\\)-intercept 25; negative cubic shape (min in 4th quadrant, max in 1st).</p>", {"figure": {"x": [-1, 6.5], "y": [-30, 40], "xTicks": 1, "yTicks": 10, "curves": [{"poly": [-2, 17, -40, 25]}], "points": [[1, 0, "1"], [2.5, 0, "2.5"], [5, 0, "5"], [0, 25, "(0, 25)"]]}}, "<p><b>(b)</b> On/below the axis: \\(1 \\leqslant x \\leqslant \\tfrac52\\) and \\(x \\geqslant 5\\).</p>"], "answer": "(b) \\(1 \\leqslant x \\leqslant \\tfrac52\\) or \\(x \\geqslant 5\\)", "markScheme": ["(a) B1 — correct shape (distinct min in 4th quadrant, max in 1st, no extra turning); B1 — 1, 2.5, 5 on the x-axis; B1 — 25 on the y-axis", "(b) B1 — \\(1 \\leqslant x \\leqslant 2.5\\); B1 — \\(x \\geqslant 5\\)"]}, {"id": "s26-p21-q1", "group": "cubic", "source": {"session": "May/June 2026", "paper": "4037/21", "question": "1"}, "calc": true, "outcomes": ["4.4", "4.5"], "topics": ["positive cubic sketch", "cubic inequality > 0"], "marks": 5, "targetMinutes": 8, "difficulty": 2, "parts": [{"label": "(a)", "text": "On the axes, sketch the graph of \\(y = \\tfrac13(2x-1)(x+3)(2x+5)\\). State the intercepts with the coordinate axes.", "marks": 3}, {"label": "(b)", "text": "Solve the inequality \\(\\tfrac13(2x-1)(x+3)(2x+5) > 0\\).", "marks": 2}], "statement": "<p><b>(a)</b> On the axes, sketch the graph of \\(\\;y = \\tfrac13(2x-1)(x+3)(2x+5)\\). State the intercepts with the coordinate axes.&emsp;<b>[3]</b></p><p><b>(b)</b> Solve the inequality \\(\\;\\tfrac13(2x-1)(x+3)(2x+5) > 0\\).&emsp;<b>[2]</b></p>", "tips": ["Roots: \\(\\tfrac12, -3, -\\tfrac52\\). \\(y\\)-intercept: \\(\\tfrac13(-1)(3)(5) = -5\\).", "Positive leading coefficient (\\(\\tfrac13\\cdot2\\cdot1\\cdot2=\\tfrac43\\)) → rises to the right (up-down-up).", "Positive cubic with roots \\(a<b<c\\) is \\(>0\\) on \\(a<x<b\\) and \\(x>c\\).", "Strict inequality → strict answer (exclude the roots)."], "solution": ["<p><b>(a)</b> Roots \\(-3, -\\tfrac52, \\tfrac12\\); \\(y\\)-intercept \\(-5\\); positive cubic (max in 2nd quadrant, min in 3rd... curve rises to the right).</p>", {"figure": {"x": [-4.5, 2], "y": [-8, 6], "xTicks": 1, "yTicks": 2, "curves": [{"poly": [1.3333333, 6.6666667, 6.3333333, -5]}], "points": [[-3, 0, "-3"], [-2.5, 0, "-2.5"], [0.5, 0, "0.5"], [0, -5, "(0, -5)"]]}}, "<p><b>(b)</b> Above the axis: \\(-3 < x < -\\tfrac52\\) and \\(x > \\tfrac12\\).</p>"], "answer": "(b) \\(-3 < x < -\\tfrac52\\) or \\(x > \\tfrac12\\)", "markScheme": ["(a) B1 — correct cubic shape, ends extending beyond the outer roots; B1 — \\(-3, -2.5, 0.5\\) on the x-axis; B1 — \\(-5\\) on the y-axis", "(b) B1 — \\(-3 < x < -\\tfrac52\\); B1 — \\(x > \\tfrac12\\) (SC1 if both non-strict)"]}, {"id": "s25-p12-q1", "group": "graph-read", "source": {"session": "May/June 2025", "paper": "4037/12", "question": "1"}, "calc": false, "outcomes": ["4.4"], "topics": ["reverse-engineer f from |f| graph"], "marks": 3, "targetMinutes": 5, "difficulty": 3, "statement": "<p>The diagram shows the graph of \\(y = |f(x)|\\), where f is a cubic polynomial. The graph meets the \\(x\\)-axis at \\(x=-2\\), \\(x=-0.5\\) and \\(x=3\\), and the \\(y\\)-axis at \\(y=2\\).</p><p>Find expressions for the two possible functions \\(f(x)\\). Write each expression in fully factorised form.&emsp;<b>[3]</b></p>", "figure": {"x": [-3.2, 4.2], "y": [-0.5, 4.5], "xTicks": 1, "yTicks": 1, "curves": [{"poly": [0.6666667, -0.3333333, -4.3333333, -2], "abs": true}], "points": [[-2, 0, "-2"], [-0.5, 0, "-0.5"], [3, 0, "3"], [0, 2, "2"]]}, "tips": ["The x-intercepts of \\(|f|\\) are the roots of f: \\(f(x) = k(x+2)(2x+1)(x-3)\\) (use \\(2x+1\\) for the root \\(-0.5\\) to keep integer coefficients).", "Fix \\(k\\) with the y-intercept: \\(|f(0)| = 2\\). Since \\(f(0) = k(2)(1)(-3) = -6k\\), you need \\(|-6k| = 2 \\Rightarrow k = \\pm\\tfrac13\\).", "TWO answers — the modulus hides the sign of f: \\(f\\) and \\(-f\\) have the same modulus graph.", "\"Fully factorised\" — leave as a product of the three linear factors times the constant."], "solution": ["<p>Roots of f at \\(-2, -\\tfrac12, 3\\) give \\(f(x) = k(x+2)(2x+1)(x-3)\\).</p>", "<p>\\(f(0) = k(2)(1)(-3) = -6k\\), and \\(|f(0)| = 2\\), so \\(6|k| = 2 \\Rightarrow k = \\pm\\tfrac13\\).</p>", "<p>The two possibilities: \\(f(x) = \\tfrac13(x+2)(2x+1)(x-3)\\) or \\(f(x) = -\\tfrac13(x+2)(2x+1)(x-3)\\).</p>"], "answer": "\\(f(x) = \\pm\\tfrac13(x+2)(2x+1)(x-3)\\)", "markScheme": ["B1 — \\(\\pm\\) with the 3 factors (or expanded cubic)", "B1 — \\(\\tfrac13\\) in a product with 3 factors", "B1 — factors \\((x+2)(2x+1)(x-3)\\) correct with no extra terms (or \\(2(x+2)(x+0.5)(x-3)\\) form... any exact equivalent)"]}, {"id": "w24-p23-q1", "group": "graph-read", "source": {"session": "Oct/Nov 2024", "paper": "4037/23", "question": "1"}, "calc": true, "outcomes": ["4.5"], "topics": ["cubic inequality f(x) < d from graph"], "marks": 3, "targetMinutes": 5, "difficulty": 2, "statement": "<p>The diagram shows the graph of \\(y = (x+1)(x-1)(x-2)\\) drawn on a grid. Use the graph to solve the inequality \\(\\;(x+1)(x-1)(x-2) < 1\\).&emsp;<b>[3]</b></p>", "figure": {"x": [-2, 3], "y": [-2, 4], "xTicks": 1, "yTicks": 1, "grid": true, "curves": [{"poly": [1, -2, -1, 2]}], "hlines": [1]}, "tips": ["The right-hand side is 1, NOT 0 — do not just read the roots! Draw the horizontal line \\(y = 1\\) and find where the curve crosses it.", "The curve is below \\(y=1\\) in two pieces: everything left of the first crossing, and the dip between the second and third crossings.", "Read crossings from the grid to 1–2 d.p. (tolerance is allowed): \\(x \\approx -0.8,\\ 0.55,\\ 2.25\\).", "Strict inequality → strict answers with <."], "solution": ["<p>Draw \\(y = 1\\). The curve \\(y=(x+1)(x-1)(x-2)\\) crosses it three times: at \\(x \\approx -0.8\\), \\(x \\approx 0.55\\) and \\(x \\approx 2.25\\).</p>", "<p>The curve is <b>below</b> the line for \\(x < -0.8\\) and between the middle pair: \\(0.55 < x < 2.25\\).</p>", "<p><i>(Algebraic check: \\(x^3-2x^2-x+1=0\\) has roots ≈ −0.80, 0.55, 2.25 ✓ — but the graphical reading is what is asked.)</i></p>"], "answer": "\\(x < -0.8\\) and \\(0.55 < x < 2.25\\) (graph readings)", "markScheme": ["B1 — the three critical values \\(-0.8, 0.55, 2.25\\) read from the graph", "B2 — \\(x < -0.8\\), \\(0.55 < x < 2.25\\) (B1 for either inequality correct)"]}]});
